        "bookmarked": false,
        "raw": "<p>Reposting from another thread, re: the Initiative system:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To me, that's an easy fix:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>1) The environment/scene tracker always goes last in initiative order.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2) The environment does not get to 'choose' who goes next; the last player (or GM) to take a turn chooses who starts the next round (following the Environment/Scene Tracker).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3) The GM may not pass initiative to themselves, even if they have multiple actors within the scene, unless all players have already gone.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>4) Any player who chooses to pass initiative to the GM may, on their next turn, reroll a single die of their choice.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yes, this doesn't \"eliminate\" the possibility of the players all going before the villain, but it does disincentivize doing so, as a player can reap a tangible, in-game benefit by passing play to the GM.&nbsp; Further, it makes GM tracking easier, by keeping the scene tracker's place in initiative static.&nbsp;</p>",
